    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Legends suggest that Ashoka was not the crown prince and took the Maurya throne around 268 BCE after a disputed succession in which he killed one or more of his brothers.</li><li>His edicts state that the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> in his eighth regnal year, with its dead and deported, made him repent violence and devote himself to dhamma, or righteous conduct.</li><li>Almost forgotten until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> was deciphered in the 19th century, he is known from his own inscriptions and from Buddhist legends that often contradict each other.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The edict in which Ashoka confesses his remorse for the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> was inscribed at several places across his empire, but it is omitted from his inscriptions found in the Kalinga region itself.</p></aside>
    </section>

